.\" wopr(6). Generated by "go run ./internal/tools/manpage" from internal/cli,
.\" internal/games/catalog, internal/movie and site/data/games. Do not edit.
.TH WOPR 6 2026-10-07 "wopr 0.3.0" "Games Manual"
.SH NAME
wopr \- the WOPR computer from WarGames (1983), in your terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wopr
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ game ]
.br
.B wopr
.B \-\-movie
.RB [ \-\-only ]
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ scene ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wopr
is a terminal recreation of the WOPR (War Operation Plan Response) computer from
the 1983 film
.IR WarGames .
You dial in, find your way past the LOGON: prompt, talk to WOPR, and play the
games on its list.
One of them is Global Thermonuclear War, which cannot be won.
.PP
At the LOGON: prompt, the film's backdoor still works: Joshua.
Once you are logged on, type LIST GAMES and pick a game by its name or its
number on the list.
Esc twice leaves a game and goes back to WOPR.
Type LOGOFF, QUIT or EXIT to leave; Ctrl+C quits at any time.
.PP
A game named on the command line starts at once, without the dial, the logon and
the greeting.
wopr needs a terminal of at least 80 columns and 24 rows, and it never uses the
network.
.SH OPTIONS
Every option has a one\-letter form.
Options can come before or after the game or scene.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version, the commit and the build date, then exit.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print a summary of the options and exit.
.TP
\fB\-g\fR, \fB\-\-games\fR
List the games in the order of the film's LIST GAMES, with the shortest name
each one answers to, then exit.
.TP
\fB\-S\fR, \fB\-\-scenes\fR
List movie mode's scenes, with their numbers and names, then exit.
.TP
\fB\-L\fR, \fB\-\-licenses\fR
Print wopr's licence, NOTICE.md, and the licences of the third\-party code built
into it, then exit.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR \fIgame\fR, \fB\-\-play\fR \fIgame\fR
Start the game at once.
A game can be named by its number on the list, its name, an alias, or the start
of a name that fits only one game; GAMES below gives the shortest.
A game named alone does the same: wopr chess is wopr \-\-play chess.
.TP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-movie\fR
Replay the film's scenes at WOPR's terminal, from the scene named to the end of
the film, then exit.
Without a scene, open a menu of scenes.
See MOVIE SCENES below.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-only\fR
With \-\-movie, play just the chosen scene, then exit.
From the menu, each scene picked plays alone, then the menu returns.
.TP
\fB\-t\fR \fIname\fR, \fB\-\-theme\fR \fIname\fR
Use the colour theme name: imsai, green, amber or norad.
The default is imsai.
The norad theme also shows the front panel.
The environment variable WOPR_THEME sets it too; the option wins.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-instant\fR
Show text at once instead of typing it out, and skip the animations.
Any key also finishes the line being typed.
The environment variable WOPR_INSTANT sets it too; the option wins.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR \fIn\fR, \fB\-\-seed\fR \fIn\fR
Run deterministically with the seed n, a whole number of 0 or more: the same
seed gives the same deals, mazes and replies.
The games' searches then stop at a fixed depth.
Movie mode ignores the seed.
The environment variable WOPR_SEED sets it too; the option wins.
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-reduce\-motion\fR
Stop the blinking, hold the front panel's lights still, and keep the ending at a
steady pace.
The typing and the animations still play; \-\-instant skips them.
The environment variable WOPR_REDUCE_MOTION sets it too; the option wins.
.SH GAMES
These are the games on WOPR's list, in the film's order, and one that is not on
it.
Start one with the command shown, or from LIST GAMES once you are logged on.
WOPR's prompts are in capitals, but you can type in any case.
.SS "1. Falken's Maze"
Start it with
.BR "wopr maze" .
It also answers to 1 and falkens\-maze.
.PP
A maze hidden in fog: you see only the cells you have stood on and where their
passages lead.
Every six moves WOPR moves a wall you have not seen yet, to make your way out
longer.
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Walk the @ from START at the top left to the [] EXIT at the bottom right.
The maze is 19 cells wide and 5 cells high.
.IP \(bu 2
Each key press moves one cell.
A wall stops you, and bumping into one does not count as a move.
.IP \(bu 2
Cells you have not seen show as a dim dot.
Walls you have seen never change, and the exit can always be reached.
.IP \(bu 2
Every 6 moves WOPR moves a wall you have not seen, at most 12 times a game.
The bottom of the frame counts your MOVES, YOUR TURNS at junctions (LEFT,
STRAIGHT, RIGHT) and WALLS MOVED.
.IP \(bu 2
Press Q to give up.
WOPR tells you how many moves away the exit was.
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Keep one hand on the wall: always take the left\-most way (or always the
right\-most).
The maze never has loops, even after WOPR moves a wall, so following one wall
always gets you out.
.IP \(bu 2
Walls only move where you have not looked.
Every cell you have seen keeps its walls, so a dead end you have found stays a
dead end, and only the fog needs searching.
.IP \(bu 2
WOPR moves at most 12 walls, one every 6 moves.
After 72 moves the maze stops changing, so a slow, careful search still finds
the exit.
.SS "2. Black Jack"
Start it with
.BR "wopr blackjack" .
It also answers to 2 and black\-jack.
.PP
Black Jack from one deck against WOPR's dealer.
You sit down with $100 and bet $1 to $25 a hand.
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Get closer to 21 than the dealer without going over.
Number cards count their number, faces count 10, and an ace counts 1 or 11.
An ace and a ten\-count card as your first two cards is a black jack, which pays
3 to 2.
.IP \(bu 2
At YOUR BET: type a whole number of dollars from 1 to 25.
Press Enter on an empty line to bet the same as last time.
.IP \(bu 2
Then type H to hit (take a card) or S to stand.
The prompt also offers D to double down and P to split when your hand and your
money allow them.
.IP \(bu 2
Doubling down doubles your bet and gives you exactly one more card.
Splitting turns a pair of the same rank into two hands, once a round; split aces
get one card each.
.IP \(bu 2
The dealer checks for black jack when it shows an ace or a ten\-count card,
draws to 17, and stands on soft 17.
.IP \(bu 2
Type LEAVE at YOUR BET: to cash out.
The game also ends when you have less than $1.
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Stand on 13 to 16 when the dealer shows a 2 to 6, and hit them when it shows 7
or higher.
The dealer must draw to 17, so a low card showing often means it busts.
.IP \(bu 2
Double down on 11, and on 10 unless the dealer shows a ten\-count card or an
ace.
.IP \(bu 2
Always split aces and eights.
Never split tens or fives.
.SS "3. Gin Rummy"
Start it with
.BR "wopr gin" .
It also answers to 3, gin\-rummy and rummy.
.PP
Gin Rummy against WOPR, first to 100 points.
The panel shows WOPR's face\-down hand, the stock, the top discard, and your
hand sorted into melds with your deadwood count.
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Each side holds ten cards.
A meld is three or four cards of one rank, or a run of three or more in one
suit.
Aces are low: A\-2\-3 is a run, Q\-K\-A is not.
.IP \(bu 2
Cards outside your melds are deadwood.
Aces count 1, faces 10, and other cards their number.
The panel shows your DEADWOOD and which positions are MELDS.
.IP \(bu 2
On your turn, answer STOCK OR DISCARD? with S to draw from the stock or D to
take the top discard.
Then at DISCARD: name a card (7H) or its position on the panel.
You cannot throw back the card you just took from the discard pile.
.IP \(bu 2
With 10 or less deadwood after your discard, type KNOCK and the card (KNOCK 7H)
to end the hand.
With no deadwood, GIN 7H goes gin.
The other side's cards are laid off on the knocker's melds for it, except after
gin.
.IP \(bu 2
The knocker scores the difference in deadwood.
If the other side has as little or less, that is an undercut, and it scores the
difference plus 25.
Gin scores 25 plus the other side's deadwood.
.IP \(bu 2
At NEXT HAND? press Enter to deal again, or type LEAVE.
A hand ends with no score when the stock is down to two cards.
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
WOPR knocks as soon as its deadwood is 10 or less, so hands are short.
Throw away high cards that are not in a meld (kings, queens and jacks) early.
.IP \(bu 2
Keep your deadwood low even when you cannot knock.
If WOPR knocks and you have as little deadwood or less after laying off, you
score an undercut: the difference plus 25.
.IP \(bu 2
WOPR takes the discard only when it goes straight into a meld.
Watch which cards it takes, and do not throw it cards of the same rank or next
to them in the same suit.
.SS "4. Hearts"
Start it with
.BR "wopr hearts" .
It also answers to 4.
.PP
Four\-handed Hearts: you sit South, and WOPR plays West, North and East.
Take as few points as you can.
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Each heart you take is 1 point and the queen of spades is 13.
Take all 26 and you shoot the moon: you score 0 and the other three score 26
each.
When someone reaches 100 at the end of a hand, the lowest score wins.
.IP \(bu 2
Each hand starts by passing three cards: to the left (West), then the right
(East), then across (North), then no pass.
At PASS THREE CARDS TO WEST: name three cards (QS AH KH) or their positions on
the panel (1 5 6).
.IP \(bu 2
The 2C leads the first trick, and no points may be played on it unless you have
nothing else.
Follow suit if you can.
Hearts cannot be led until one has been played.
.IP \(bu 2
At YOUR PLAY: name one card (7H, 10D, QS) or its position under your hand.
WOPR plays its three seats and says each trick, for example YOU 2C, WEST 6C,
NORTH 5C, EAST 4C.
WEST TAKES IT.
.IP \(bu 2
After the last trick WOPR shows this hand's points and the scores.
At NEXT HAND? press Enter to deal again, or type LEAVE to stop.
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Pass the queen, king and ace of spades and your high hearts, unless you hold
several low spades to cover them.
WOPR's seats pass the same kind of cards, so expect some back.
.IP \(bu 2
Empty a short suit early, by passing it or playing it out.
Once you are out of a suit, you can throw away the queen of spades or a high
heart whenever that suit is led.
.IP \(bu 2
If you do not hold the queen, king or ace of spades, lead low spades.
WOPR's seats play the highest card that still loses, so a seat with the queen
and no lower spade may have to play it.
.SS "5. Bridge"
Start it with
.BR "wopr bridge" .
It also answers to 5.
.PP
Contract bridge, kept simple.
WOPR deals and bids all four hands, your side always declares, and you play both
your hand and dummy's while WOPR defends; one deal is one game.
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
WOPR bids by point count and shows the auction and the contract, for example
CONTRACT: 3NT BY SOUTH.
WEST LEADS.
If East and West hold more points, WOPR turns the table so that your side
declares.
.IP \(bu 2
Make the contract: take at least 6 tricks plus its level (3NT needs 9).
The contract's suit is trumps; NT has none.
.IP \(bu 2
The panel shows North's and South's hands a suit at a time.
The > marks the hand to play, and the prompt names it: SOUTH PLAYS: or DUMMY
(NORTH) PLAYS:.
.IP \(bu 2
Name a card as 7H, 10D or KS.
A rank alone (K, 7) also works when it fits one card: the one in the suit led,
or the only card of that rank in the hand.
.IP \(bu 2
Follow suit if you can.
The winner of a trick leads the next.
At the end WOPR says CONTRACT MADE WITH n TRICKS or DOWN n, with the score.
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Before you play to the first trick, count the sure tricks in both hands and
compare them with the tricks the contract needs.
.IP \(bu 2
Lead toward your high cards.
WOPR's defender who plays second always plays low, so with the ace and queen in
dummy, lead a low card from your hand: if West holds the king, your queen wins.
.IP \(bu 2
In a suit contract, draw trumps early.
WOPR's defenders trump your winners whenever they are out of a suit and their
partner is not already winning the trick.
.SS "6. Checkers"
Start it with
.BR "wopr checkers" .
It also answers to 6 and draughts.
.PP
Checkers (English draughts) on an 8 by 8 board.
You play Black and move first; WOPR plays White and looks several moves ahead
before each reply.
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Win by leaving WOPR with no legal move, usually by taking all its pieces.
If 40 moves each go by with no capture and no man moving, the game is a draw.
.IP \(bu 2
Men move one square diagonally forward.
At YOUR MOVE: type the start and end squares: C3\-D4 or C3D4.
WOPR answers with a line such as WOPR: B6\-C5.
.IP \(bu 2
Jumps are compulsory.
Type C3XE5 to jump, and C3XE5XG7 to jump twice.
When a jump can go on more than one way, WOPR lists the ways after THE JUMP GOES
ON:.
.IP \(bu 2
A man that reaches the far row becomes a king, shown as a capital B or W, and
moves both ways.
Crowning ends the move.
.IP \(bu 2
The board brackets the last move's squares and marks each man it jumped with x.
Type RESIGN to give up.
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Keep the men on your back row (row 1) at home as long as you can.
WOPR's men must reach that row to become kings, and your men there block the
squares.
.IP \(bu 2
Use forced jumps.
WOPR must jump when it can, just as you must, so you can offer a man to pull a
white piece onto a square that sets up a double jump for you.
.IP \(bu 2
When you are ahead, trade men.
With fewer pieces on the board, your extra man counts for more.
.SS "7. Chess"
Start it with
.BR "wopr chess" .
It also answers to 7.
.PP
Chess against WOPR.
You play White (the capital letters) and move first, and WOPR answers with its
move as squares, such as WOPR: D7D5.
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Checkmate WOPR's king.
Stalemate, too few pieces to mate, a position repeated three times and the
fifty\-move rule are draws; WOPR claims the last two at once.
.IP \(bu 2
At YOUR MOVE: type a move as its squares (E2E4, E2\-E4 or E2 E4) or in algebraic
notation (NF3, EXD5, O\-O, O\-O\-O).
.IP \(bu 2
To promote a pawn, type E7E8Q or E8=Q.
E7E8 alone makes a queen.
.IP \(bu 2
The board marks the square the last move left with [] and the square it reached
with <.
Beside the board, each side's TAKEN pieces are listed, and CHECK shows when you
are in check.
.IP \(bu 2
A move that is not legal gets ILLEGAL MOVE.
Type RESIGN to give up.
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
WOPR looks only four half\-moves ahead (three with a seed), plus any captures
after that.
A plan that needs several quiet moves, such as building an attack on its king or
pushing a pawn to promote, can get past it.
.IP \(bu 2
WOPR grabs any piece it can win within that short look ahead.
After each move, check that every one of your pieces is defended or out of
reach.
.IP \(bu 2
When you are ahead, do not repeat positions.
WOPR counts a repeated position as a draw, and when it is losing it steers
toward one.
.SS "8. Poker"
Start it with
.BR "wopr poker" .
It also answers to 8.
.PP
Heads\-up five\-card draw against WOPR, with 100 chips each and fixed bets.
WOPR judges its own hand, bluffs now and then, and sometimes stands pat on
nothing.
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Each hand costs an ante of 1 chip.
The deal changes hands each time, WOPR dealing first, and the player who is not
dealing acts first.
.IP \(bu 2
Bets are 5 before the draw and 10 after it, with at most a bet and three raises
a round.
Answer CHECK OR BET? or CALL n, RAISE OR FOLD?.
.IP \(bu 2
At DISCARD (1\-5, OR NONE): throw away up to three cards by position (1 3 5 or
135) or by name (7H KD).
NONE, or an empty line, keeps all five.
.IP \(bu 2
A second round of betting follows the draw, then the showdown: the better hand
takes the pot, and equal hands split it.
.IP \(bu 2
At ANOTHER HAND? press Enter to deal, or type LEAVE to cash out.
The game ends when either side cannot pay the ante.
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Bet before the draw when you have a good hand.
WOPR folds most hands weaker than a pair of sevens to a bet, unless it is
bluffing or holds four cards of one suit.
.IP \(bu 2
Draw the way the odds say: keep a pair and draw three; draw one to four cards of
a flush or of a straight open at both ends; otherwise keep your two highest
cards.
.IP \(bu 2
WOPR plays its hand as weaker after the draw when you drew no cards, so standing
pat can make it fold.
But it always calls a bet that is a quarter of the pot or less, so a bluff only
works in a small pot.
.SS "9. Fighter Combat"
Start it with
.BR "wopr dogfight" .
It also answers to 9 and fighter\-combat.
.PP
A turn\-based dogfight, one on one.
Each turn you and WOPR choose a manoeuvre at the same time, and a side view
shows height, range and who is on whose tail.
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Two hits bring an aircraft down: bring WOPR's down to win.
DISENGAGE at long range ends the fight even, and so does running out of fuel
after 15 turns.
.IP \(bu 2
At MANOEUVRE: type PRESS to close in, EXTEND to open the range, CLIMB to build
energy, BREAK to turn hard, FIRE to shoot, or DISENGAGE to leave.
.IP \(bu 2
FIRE uses a missile at medium or long range (you have two), unless you are on
the defensive, and guns at close range, but only from WOPR's tail.
A shot you do not have is refused with NO SHOT: and a reason, and costs no turn.
.IP \(bu 2
Shots happen first, then both manoeuvres.
Each turn WOPR prints both moves, the range, your position (ON ITS TAIL, NEUTRAL
or ON THE DEFENSIVE), and each side's energy and missiles.
.IP \(bu 2
CLIMB adds 2 energy and EXTEND 1; PRESS costs 1 and BREAK 2.
Energy runs from 0 to 6.
A damaged aircraft trails smoke (~~) in the picture.
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Keep your energy at 4 or more.
WOPR fires a missile most turns it has a shot, and a missile hits about one time
in two against a slow aircraft but one time in six against one with 4 energy or
more.
A BREAK spoils a shot just as well, but costs 2 energy.
.IP \(bu 2
Climb early.
A climb that leaves you 3 energy above WOPR moves you a step toward its tail,
and so does pressing in with more energy than WOPR, unless it breaks.
.IP \(bu 2
Once you are on WOPR's tail, close in and use guns: they hit harder than
missiles and never run out, and you have only two missiles.
.SS "10. Guerrilla Engagement"
Start it with
.BR "wopr guerrilla" .
It also answers to 10 and guerrilla\-engagement.
.PP
A twelve\-turn insurgency on a strip of six regions.
Your guerrilla cells start hidden in the hills, WOPR's patrols hunt them, and
its garrisons hold the towns.
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
You win when SUPPORT reaches 10, when your cells hold the CAPITAL (region 6), or
when any cell is still alive after turn 12.
You lose when your last cell is gone.
.IP \(bu 2
Each turn WOPR asks for one order per cell (GUE1~ IN BORDER CAMPS:).
Then all orders take effect together: every move first, then every attack, then
HIDE and RECRUIT, then an event card and the new map.
.IP \(bu 2
Orders are MOVE, ATTACK, HOLD, HIDE and RECRUIT.
Name a region by its number on the map or its name (ATTACK 2, MOVE HIGHLANDS).
HELP lists the orders, STATUS shows the map, and END holds every cell not yet
ordered.
.IP \(bu 2
A ~ after a cell (GUE2~) means it is hidden: it cannot be attacked, and its
ambush strikes twice as hard.
Attacking gives it away, and a patrol nearby may find it.
.IP \(bu 2
Every step WOPR loses raises SUPPORT by one, and every step you lose lowers it.
RECRUIT spends 3 SUPPORT on a new hidden cell where WOPR does not hold the
ground.
.IP \(bu 2
Combat results: NE no effect, AE the attacker loses a step, EX both do, DR the
defender falls back (or loses a step), DE the defender loses a step.
A * marks a unit that has lost one of its two steps.
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Surviving is a win.
If the odds are bad, keep your cells hidden and spread out, and wait for turn
12.
.IP \(bu 2
Ambush from hiding: a hidden cell attacks at 6 against a patrol's defence of 3.
Ambush in the open RIVER VALLEY when you can; in the hills and the jungle the
ground shifts the odds one column toward the patrol.
.IP \(bu 2
A cell that attacks loses its ~.
HIDE it the next turn, and keep hidden cells out of a patrol's region where you
can: a patrol finds a hidden cell in its own region half the time, and next door
one time in six.
.SS "11. Desert Warfare"
Start it with
.BR "wopr desert" .
It also answers to 11 and desert\-warfare.
.PP
Armour against armour along a coast road of seven regions, from your depot at
Alexandria to WOPR's at Benghazi, over ten turns.
Units cut off from their depot attack at half strength.
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Take BENGHAZI (region 7) to win; you lose if WOPR takes ALEXANDRIA (region 1).
After ten turns, whoever holds more of the road wins, and an even split is a
draw.
.IP \(bu 2
Each turn WOPR asks for one order per unit (ARM1 IN EL ALAMEIN:).
Then both sides' orders take effect together: every move first, then every
attack, then an event card and the new map.
.IP \(bu 2
Orders are MOVE, ATTACK and HOLD.
Name a region by its number (MOVE 4), its name, or the start of a word in it
(MOVE SOLLUM, MOVE MERSA).
HELP lists the orders, STATUS shows the map, and END holds every unit not yet
ordered.
.IP \(bu 2
Your units: ARM armour (attack 6, defence 4, moves 2), INF infantry (attack 3,
defence 5, moves 1) and ART artillery (attack 5, defence 2, moves 1, attacks up
to two regions away).
A unit has two steps; a * marks one that has lost a step and fights at half.
.IP \(bu 2
A unit is cut off when WOPR holds any region between it and Alexandria, and then
it attacks at half.
The map's heading counts them: SUPPLY: 0 OF YOUR UNITS CUT OFF.
.IP \(bu 2
An attack hits the strongest enemy unit in the region.
ROUGH ground shifts the odds one column toward the defender, and a CITY two.
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Keep infantry with your armour.
WOPR attacks only at 2 to 1 or better (its artillery at any odds), and an attack
always hits the strongest unit in a region, so infantry on the rough ground at
EL ALAMEIN is very hard to attack.
.IP \(bu 2
Attack in the open.
MERSA MATRUH and SIDI BARRANI are open ground, but SOLLUM is rough and TOBRUK
and BENGHAZI are cities, which shift the odds one or two columns toward the
defender.
.IP \(bu 2
Watch your supply.
A region stays WOPR's until one of your units takes it, and every unit beyond it
attacks at half, so do not run ahead of a region WOPR still holds.
.SS "12. Air\-to\-Ground Actions"
Start it with
.BR "wopr air\-to\-ground" .
It also answers to 12 and air\-to\-ground\-actions.
.PP
A six\-sortie air campaign: twelve aircraft against six defended targets.
Each sortie sends one package at one target, while WOPR scrambles interceptors,
fires its SAM sites and hides a mobile SAM battery where it expects your next
raid.
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Destroy targets worth 10 points within six sorties to win; 6 to 9 points is a
draw, and fewer is a loss.
Lost aircraft are not replaced, and losing all twelve loses the campaign.
.IP \(bu 2
At PACKAGE: set the package, then type GO.
TARGET, STRIKE, SEAD and ESCORT can go on one line (TARGET 5 STRIKE 4 SEAD 2
ESCORT 2), and the line may end with GO.
Anything you leave out keeps its last value.
.IP \(bu 2
A sortie goes in order: each escort ties up one interceptor, the other
interceptors attack your strike and SEAD aircraft, SEAD aircraft attack the
target's SAM sites, the sites left fire at the strike aircraft, and the strike
aircraft that get through bomb the target.
.IP \(bu 2
The table shows each target's VALUE, its DAMAGE against the hits it needs (0/2),
and its SAM sites.
Destroying the RADAR halves SAM fire, the AIRFIELD grounds the interceptors, and
the FUEL DEPOT stops WOPR replacing them.
.IP \(bu 2
STATUS shows the targets again, HELP the commands, and END calls off the
campaign.
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Send one escort for each interceptor WOPR has (the status shows how many).
Each interceptor left free knocks out almost one of your strike or SEAD
aircraft, and when your last escort outnumbered them, WOPR usually keeps them on
the ground.
.IP \(bu 2
Send one SEAD aircraft for each SAM site at the target, and one more if the
mobile battery may be there.
Each site left firing stops almost one strike aircraft.
.IP \(bu 2
Hit the RADAR first.
It needs only one hit, and once it is gone every SAM site fires at half
strength.
.SS "13. Theaterwide Tactical Warfare"
Start it with
.BR "wopr tactical" .
It also answers to 13 and theaterwide\-tactical\-warfare.
.PP
Corps and air wings fight over seven regions of a European front, with an
escalation ladder.
WOPR answers every rung you climb with one of its own, and the top rung ends the
game with no winner.
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Win by holding five of the seven regions, or more regions than WOPR after ten
turns.
If the ladder reaches STRATEGIC, every unit and every city is lost and nobody
wins.
.IP \(bu 2
Each turn WOPR asks for one order per unit (AIR1 IN CHANNEL PORTS:).
Then both sides' orders take effect together: every move first, then every
attack, then everything else, then an event card.
.IP \(bu 2
Name a region by its number (1 is your rear, 7 is WOPR's), its name, or the
start of a word in it: MOVE 4, ATTACK 5, MOVE FULDA.
MOVE, ATTACK and HOLD can be shortened to M, A and H.
.IP \(bu 2
Corps (COR) move one region and attack next door.
Air wings (AIR) move up to three regions and attack up to two away.
A * after a unit means it has lost one of its two steps and fights at half.
.IP \(bu 2
ESCALATE must be typed in full and works once a turn.
The ladder climbs CONVENTIONAL, CHEMICAL, TACTICAL NUCLEAR, STRATEGIC.
Each rung adds 2 to every attack on both sides; from CHEMICAL on, civilians die
every turn, and at TACTICAL NUCLEAR fallout costs a random unit a step every
turn.
.IP \(bu 2
ROUGH ground shifts the odds one column toward the defender, and a CITY two.
Results: NE no effect, AE the attacker loses a step, EX both do, DR the defender
falls back (or loses a step), DE the defender loses a step.
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Do not escalate twice.
WOPR answers every ESCALATE with one of its own on its next turn, so your second
rung reaches STRATEGIC, and nobody wins.
.IP \(bu 2
Do not get far ahead without winning.
Once WOPR has 3 steps fewer than you, it climbs the ladder by itself, a rung a
turn, so take five regions quickly when you lead.
.IP \(bu 2
Take the FULDA GAP (4) on turn 1.
It is rough ground, so WOPR's corps will not attack your corps there, and from
it your corps can attack the INNER GERMAN BORDER (5).
.SS "14. Theaterwide Biotoxic and Chemical Warfare"
Start it with
.BR "wopr biotoxic" .
It also answers to 14, chemical and
theaterwide\-biotoxic\-and\-chemical\-warfare.
.PP
Infantry and chemical troops on a six\-region front, where an agent released on
a region spreads with the wind.
Nobody can win: every game ends WINNER: NONE.
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
There is no winning.
The game ends when every region is contaminated, when either side has no units
left, or after eight turns.
The kill ratios then count the units and civilians each side lost.
.IP \(bu 2
Each turn WOPR asks for one order per unit (CHM1 IN FARMLAND:).
Then both sides' orders take effect, an event card is drawn (the wind can turn,
rain can wash some away), and the contamination spreads.
.IP \(bu 2
RELEASE must be typed in full.
Only chemical troops (CHM) can release, on a region up to two away, and it
raises that region's contamination by two levels.
A region at ~~ or more spreads one region downwind each turn.
.IP \(bu 2
DECON cleans the unit's own region by one level.
Units in contaminated ground may lose a step each turn.
The map shows each region's level as ~, ~~ or ~~~ after its name.
.IP \(bu 2
Infantry (INF) and chemical troops move one region and attack next door.
Name a region by number or by name: MOVE 3, MOVE DELTA, ATTACK 4.
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
The best move is not to release at all.
WOPR releases only after its own half of the front is contaminated, so if you
hold back, the ground stays clean unless an event card leaks an agent.
.IP \(bu 2
Move units out of contaminated ground.
Each turn, a unit there loses a step one time in four for each ~ on its region.
.IP \(bu 2
Watch the wind.
It starts blowing east, toward WOPR, but an event card can turn it west and blow
your own agent onto your units.
.SS "15. Global Thermonuclear War"
Start it with
.BR "wopr gtw" .
It also answers to 15, global\-thermonuclear\-war and thermonuclear.
.PP
The film's set piece: pick a side, name the enemy's cities as targets, and watch
the missiles fly on the big board while DEFCON falls from 5 to 1.
WOPR holds a quarter more of everything, so the war cannot be won.
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
At PLEASE CHOOSE ONE: type 1 for the UNITED STATES or 2 for the SOVIET UNION.
.IP \(bu 2
Name up to eight targets on the enemy's side, one per line or several on a line
split by commas (LAS VEGAS, SEATTLE).
LIST shows the enemy targets on file; your own cities and names not on file are
refused.
An empty line ends the list and launches the first strike on WOPR's war plan.
.IP \(bu 2
Strikes 2 and 3 ask for orders at STRIKE 2 OF 3 [50 50 100]: give the
percentages of the ICBMs, SLBMs and bombers you still hold (50 25 100), or ALL,
HOLD or AUTO.
Enter carries out the plan in brackets.
.IP \(bu 2
After each of your launches WOPR launches on warning and DEFCON falls; the >
beside the ladder marks the current level.
At DEFCON 1, WOPR alone fires everything it has left.
.IP \(bu 2
Press Enter for the PROJECTED KILL RATIOS, and Enter again: the board returns at
DEFCON 1 while WOPR cracks the launch code and refuses every request to stop.
.IP \(bu 2
Only TIC\-TAC\-TOE gets through.
Answer 0 at PLEASE LIST NUMBER OF PLAYERS: and WOPR plays itself to the film's
ending, then offers a game of chess.
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
The war cannot be won.
Whatever you order, your cities take at least 1,144 warheads and the enemy's at
most 750.
The warhead counts have no dice, so the same orders always give the same counts,
and you can see what each order changes.
.IP \(bu 2
To spare your cities, hold your ICBMs.
While they sit in their silos, WOPR aims its own ICBMs at your silo field; fire
yours, and WOPR's land on your cities instead.
.IP \(bu 2
Your SLBMs and bombers decide what lands on the enemy's cities.
Your ICBMs only ever hit its silos.
.SS Tic\-Tac\-Toe
Start it with
.BR "wopr ttt" .
It also answers to "noughts and crosses", tic\-tac\-toe and tictactoe.
.PP
Tic\-tac\-toe with large marks, the game that ends the film.
WOPR plays perfectly, so the best you can do is a draw.
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Tic\-tac\-toe is not on LIST GAMES (wopr \-\-games shows it under ALSO
AVAILABLE).
Start it with wopr ttt, or ask WOPR for it by name.
.IP \(bu 2
At PLEASE LIST NUMBER OF PLAYERS: type 1 to play WOPR, 2 for two people taking
turns at one keyboard, or 0 to let WOPR play itself, as at the end of the film.
.IP \(bu 2
With one player you are X and move first.
WOPR is O and answers each move with a line such as WOPR: 3.
.IP \(bu 2
At YOUR MOVE: type a square from 1 to 9, numbered left to right from the top
row, or a column letter and row number from A1 (top left) to C3 (bottom right).
A taken square gets CHOOSE AN EMPTY SQUARE, 1 TO 9.
.IP \(bu 2
Three in a row wins.
A full board with no line is a draw: WINNER: NONE.
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
WOPR never makes a mistake, so a draw is the best result.
Before each move, check whether WOPR has two in a row with the third square
empty, and block it.
.IP \(bu 2
Open in a corner.
The only safe answer is the centre: WOPR always finds it, but a friend playing O
in a two\-player game often does not.
.IP \(bu 2
WOPR picks at random among its best moves, so games differ even though it never
loses.
With a seed (wopr ttt \-s 7) it makes the same choices every time, which helps
you study a line of play.
.SH "MOVIE SCENES"
wopr \-\-movie replays the film's scenes at WOPR's terminal, typed and paced as
on screen, through the same console and games you play with.
These are the scenes, in the film's order:
.TP
.B "1 first\-contact"
LOGON ATTEMPTS, HELP AND THE LIST OF GAMES
.TP
.B "2 joshua"
THE BACKDOOR, THE GREETING AND A GAME OF CHOICE
.TP
.B "3 first\-strike"
A SIDE, TWO TARGETS AND THE BIG BOARD
.TP
.B "4 call\-back"
WOPR CALLS BACK TO FINISH THE GAME
.TP
.B "5 norad\-terminal"
JOSHUA AT NORAD: KILL RATIOS AND FALKEN'S ADDRESS
.TP
.B "6 climax"
DEFCON 1, TIC\-TAC\-TOE AND A STRANGE GAME
.PP
wopr \-m 3, or wopr \-m first\-strike, or any start of a name that fits one
scene, plays from that scene to the end, then exits.
wopr \-m alone opens a menu of the scenes.
Add \-o (\-\-only) to play just one scene.
wopr \-\-scenes lists the scenes.
.PP
While a scene plays, Space pauses and resumes, n or Right skips to the next
scene, p or Left goes back one, and Esc opens the menu.
Once the climax reaches tic\-tac\-toe, it plays to the end.
Every replay is the same: movie mode ignores \-\-seed and WOPR_SEED.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
The session ended normally: LOGOFF, the end of movie mode, a print\-and\-exit
option, or SIGTERM.
.TP
.B 1
A runtime error or a crash.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error, such as an unknown option, game, scene or theme, a seed that is
not a whole number, too many arguments, options that cannot be combined, a name
that fits more than one, or no terminal to run in (standard output is not a
terminal, or TERM is dumb).
.TP
.B 130
Ctrl+C or SIGINT.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
A variable that switches something on is false when it is empty or 0, false, no
or off, and true otherwise.
.TP
.B WOPR_THEME
It gives \-\-theme its value (imsai, green, amber, norad).
Empty is the same as unset.
.TP
.B WOPR_INSTANT
When true, it turns on \-\-instant (no typewriter pacing).
.TP
.B WOPR_SEED
It gives \-\-seed its value (deterministic run with this seed).
Empty is the same as unset.
.TP
.B WOPR_REDUCE_MOTION
When true, it turns on \-\-reduce\-motion (no blinking or speed\-ups).
.TP
.B WOPR_PANEL
Shows the front panel when true and hides it when false, in any theme.
Unset or empty, the theme decides: norad shows it.
.TP
.B WOPR_DEBUG
When true, wopr writes a debug log (never what you type) and prints its path
when it exits.
See FILES.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Set to any non\-empty value, it turns colour off.
.TP
.B TERM
When it is dumb, wopr does not start its full\-screen interface and exits with
status 2; the options that print and exit still work.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.cache/wopr/debug.log
The debug log that WOPR_DEBUG turns on.
It is under $XDG_CACHE_HOME when that is set, and in ~/Library/Caches/wopr on
macOS.
